general_comments.body:
> Possibly related to @ljharb's comments, what kind of `devEngines` will we add to `npm/node-semver`, and how would we change the CI? Or will we intentionally not add one for the time being?

Because we're still operating on a widely popular runtime / packageManager I don't see much value in adding it, but if we were to add `devEngines` to `npm-semver` it would need to match [what we have in CI](https://github.com/npm/node-semver/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml#L48). Something like this:

```json
{
  "devEngines": {
    "runtime": {
      "name": "node",
      "version": ">10.0.0",
      "onFail": "error"
    },
    "packageManager": {
      "name": "npm",
      "version": ">10.0.0",
      "onFail": "error"
    }
  }
}
```

@ljharb 

> @reggi I agree that `devEngines` mustn't apply in CI - but since it must apply locally, there must be a way to _ignore_ devEngines (or, union with `engines`) in CI in order to achieve either goal.

I never said `devEngines` mustn't apply in CI. Running package scripts in CI is the same as you running them locally. I can't imagine people remembering to add a `--ignore-dev-engines` flag, it's an extra step I don't think is necessary. Just don't limit your `devEngines` to the point ci would fail.

